# What never leaves this machine.
#
# Every line answers one of two questions: does this file hold something a person
# would regret publishing, and is it the *source* of the work or something derived
# from it. A run's evidence and a graph over the tree are both worth keeping — on
# the machine that produced them, where a reviewer can open them.

# ── Secrets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `secrets.local.yaml` carries the credentials a run spends. `secrets.example.yaml`
# is the template that ships; the `*.local.yaml` shape is general on purpose, so a
# second local override cannot be added later and forgotten here.
secrets.local.yaml
*.local.yaml
# The exception is the parity target's: `fixture-token` is the value the fixture's
# own filter accepts and the one `tests/test_spring_fixture.py` asserts, so it is
# fixture data — not a credential — and the demo cannot start without it.
!examples/spring-fixture/secrets.local.yaml
.env
.env.*

# ── A run's evidence ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Written by the harness, read by a person, never edited. A run is replayable from
# its round plus the secrets, so versioning it buys nothing and costs whatever the
# request and response bodies happen to carry. Unanchored, so the examples' runs go
# too: their `summary.json` records the absolute path of the machine that ran them.
runs/

# ── A reviewer's write-up ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `analysis.md` is a note *about* a run, written after reading one: it describes a
# moment, not the harness. Anchored to the root, because an unanchored
# `analysis-*.md` also swallows the worked example a documentation page links to.
/analysis.md
/analysis-*.md

# ── Python ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.venv/
.venv*
venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
dist/
build/

# ── The desktop client's toolchain ────────────────────────────────────────────
# `node_modules/` is not a build artifact of this project; it is a copy of the
# registry, and `bin/audit-remote` reads every file `git ls-files -co` reports. Left
# unignored it would enter the published set, be scanned line by line for product
# tokens and copied file by file into the audit tree, for a dependency list nobody
# reviews. The *built* bundle is the opposite: it is committed on purpose (under
# `src/heimdall_qa/desktop/webapp/`) so `pip install` never runs npm, so nothing here
# may ignore it.
node_modules/
.npm/
.pnpm-store/
# Vite's caches. The output directory is `src/heimdall_qa/desktop/webapp/` on purpose,
# both to escape this rule and because the wheel has to ship it.
desktop/node_modules/
desktop/webapp/node_modules/
desktop/webapp/.vite/
desktop/webapp/coverage/
desktop/webapp/playwright-report/
desktop/webapp/test-results/

# ── The Java parity target ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `examples/spring-fixture/` is source; `target/` is that source compiled into 21 MB
# and `logs/` is what the last run wrote. The Maven wrapper needs no jar committed:
# `.mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.properties` is the whole of it, and it fetches the rest.
#
# `DISCOVERY.md` is a report, not a document: `heimdall-qa discover` writes it, and it
# records the absolute paths of the machine that ran it. Regenerate it, never commit it.
examples/spring-fixture/target/
examples/spring-fixture/logs/
examples/spring-fixture/DISCOVERY.md
*.jar
*.log

# ── The knowledge graph over the tree ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Regenerable with `graphify update .`; megabytes of JSON and HTML that would drown
# every diff.
graphify-out/

# ── Everything the project under review owns ──────────────────────────────────
# Requirement, not convention: *nothing* belonging to the project being tested may
# reach the remote. Heimdall QA is the deliverable; the product it was built against
# is one checkout that answers its questions, and it stays on this machine.
#
# Each line is a whole tree rather than a list of file names. A nominal list ages:
# the next case file, the next campaign, the next study is added and nobody
# remembers to add a line, so the leak is silent. A tree is a boundary.
#
# The paths are anchored, and that is load-bearing: an unanchored `qa/` would also
# swallow `tests/fixtures/qa/`, which is the core's own descriptor fixture and has
# to ship. `/providers/` is the cut the suite already knows how to survive — it
# passes with every provider uninstalled, which is claim 5 of
# `examples/toy-provider/gate.sh`.
#
# `bin/audit-remote` is the gate that keeps this honest; a grep only catches the
# names someone thought of, so the gate asserts the absence of the *trees* and the
# suite is run without them.
/providers/
/qa/
/docs/

# The product's own paper: the constitution it was built under, and the skill that
# carries its campaign recipe. A product's skill belongs beside its provider, and
# these three copies are regenerated by `bin/sync-skills` from the source one.
.agents/rules/
.agents/skills/heimdall-qa-round/
.cursor/skills/heimdall-qa-round/
.kiro/skills/heimdall-qa-round/

# ── OS / editor ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*~
